{"assets":["/editorial-assets/c992a3c54d37c25ac87a57e4833918066ee7611e15908a82aa5a16868786abcb.png"],"contestId":"65","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem A"}],"kind":"strong"},{"children":[{"kind":"line_break"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Fist, consider the case when all the numbers are non-zero. You can take a*c*e grams of sand, turn them into b*c*e grams of lead, and them turn into b*d*e grams of gold, and them turn into b*d*f grams of sand. If b*d*f > a*c*e, you can infinitely increase the amount of sand and consequently the amount of gold. Otherwise, if b*d*f <= a*c*e, it is impossible to get infinitely large amount of gold. The same is true when the fraction  (a*c*e)/(b*d*f) has zero numerator only or zero denuminator only. Otherwise there is 0/0, and  you have to check cases specially."}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"line_break"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"I explain, why I make this problem to be problem A."}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"line_break"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1. Solution of the problem requires very little knowledge of programming (only \"if\"), so it was feasible for all participants."}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2. Tricky cases really exist, but I hoped that \"stronger competitors will ... help newcomers to catch all bugs by their hacks\". And so it happened."}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"line_break"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Maybe I was wrong: this problem is too complex for A."}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"line_break"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A large number of hacks for this problem is a quite foreseen circumstance. I haven't include the special class of tests into the pretests intendently, to make hacking more interesting. In fairness I should note that Artem Rakhov was against this idea. Beause of this task the problem surfaced that Gassa writes about "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/comments/1432#comment-26176"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"here"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":" in Russian: pretests cover some incomplete set of cases and it is not clear in advance what the event \"solution passed pretests\" means. I think it should be discussed, and strong principles of pretests development should be stated."}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"line_break"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem B"}],"kind":"strong"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"line_break"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The problem has greedy solution. Change a digit in the first date to make it as small as possible. At each of the next steps try all one-digit exchanges in the current date and choose one that makes the date not less than the previous one and as small as possible at the same time. If the last year won't exceed 2011 - the answer is found. Otherwise there is no solution."}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"line_break"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"How to prove that? Consider any correct answer. Clearly, if the first date in it is different from the smallest possible number that can be obtained from the first of given years, your can put the smallest number instead of it. The second date can be exchanged by the smallest possible number not exceeding the first number, etc.Ar a result you obtain a greedy solution that is built by the algorithm described above. If the greedy solution doesn't exist, solution doesn't esist too."}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"line_break"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The problem also has a dynamic solution."}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"line_break"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem C"}],"kind":"strong"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"line_break"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Check for each segment in the polyline if Harry can catch the snitch in it. Note that since "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≥ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", if Harry can catch the snitch at some moment of time, he can catch it at any later moment. He can just follow snitch along its trajectory. So use binary search in each segment."}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"line_break"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"About problems with EPS that participants got. The problems may be caused by"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1) some EPS in the condition: while (leftTime + EPS < rightTime) <do binary search>. The task was to output with a fixed accurancy not only the time, but also the point coordinates. If the difference between the time and the right one is less than EPS, the difference between coordinates may be significantly larger. In such cases you should compare not arguments of a function, but values of the function with EPS. Or the way that I usually follow and that once was not liked by the teacher of numerical methods - to implement some fixed number of iterations of the binary search (say, 60).  "}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2) EPS in comparison of time moments inside the binary search like this:"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"if (currentTime + addTime > distance / potterSpeed - EPS)"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"                                                rightTime = currentTime;"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"                                        else"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"                                                leftTime = currentTime;"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Here it is better to remove EPS at all. Or to put EPS significantly smaller than the required accurancy for printing the point."}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"line_break"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"(Code fragments are from Egor's solution.)"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"line_break"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Thus, there was no crime with severe limitations. Probably for such problems extreme accurancy tests should be in pretests? It is about principles of pretests making again. "}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"kind":"line_break"}],"kind":"container"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"UPD."}],"kind":"strong"},{"kind":"text","text":" The problem has an analytical solution too. Consider the i-th segment. Let ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"z"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") and ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":